This is a linear programming problem. We find the maximum value of the objective function \(f(X,Y) = 3X + 6Y\) subject to the constraints: \[2X + Y \le 6\] \[X + 2Y \le 8\] Since \(X, Y\) are positive real numbers, we look at the intersection point of the boundary lines: \[\begin{aligned}
2X + Y &amp;= 6 \quad \text{--- (1)} \\
X + 2Y &amp;= 8 \quad \text{--- (2)}
\end{aligned}\] Multiply equation (1) by 2: \[4X + 2Y = 12\] Subtract equation (2) from this result: \[3X = 4 \implies X = \dfrac{4}{3}\] Substitute \(X = \dfrac{4}{3}\) into equation (1): \[2\left(\dfrac{4}{3}\right) + Y = 6 \implies Y = 6 - \dfrac{8}{3} = \dfrac{10}{3}\] The intersection corner point is \(\left(\dfrac{4}{3}, \dfrac{10}{3}\right)\). Let us check the objective function value at this vertex: <ul><li>At \(\left(\dfrac{4}{3}, \dfrac{10}{3}\right)\): \(f(X,Y) = 3\left(\dfrac{4}{3}\right) + 6\left(\dfrac{10}{3}\right) = 4 + 20 = 24\).</li></ul> \[\boxed{\text{A:  (4/3, 10/3)}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Corner Point Theorem</strong>: In linear programming, the maximum or minimum value of an objective function always occurs at one of the corner vertices of the feasible region.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not choose points like \(\left(\dfrac{8}{3}, \dfrac{10}{3}\right)\) without verifying if they violate the problem constraints (\(2(8/3) + 10/3 = 26/3 &gt; 6\)).</li></ul></div>

To solve the summation, we rationalize the denominator of the general term: \[T_n = \frac{1}{\sqrt{n} + \sqrt{n+1}}\] Multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate \((\sqrt{n} - \sqrt{n+1})\): \[T_n = \frac{\sqrt{n} - \sqrt{n+1}}{n - (n+1)} = \frac{\sqrt{n} - \sqrt{n+1}}{-1} = \sqrt{n+1} - \sqrt{n}\] Expanding the series from \(n=1\) to \(n=80\) creates a telescoping sum: \[\begin{aligned}
S &amp;= (\sqrt{2} - \sqrt{1}) + (\sqrt{3} - \sqrt{2}) + (\sqrt{4} - \sqrt{3}) + \dots + (\sqrt{81} - \sqrt{80})
\end{aligned}\] All intermediate terms cancel out perfectly, leaving only the first negative and last positive terms: \[S = \sqrt{81} - \sqrt{1} = 9 - 1 = 8\] \[\boxed{\text{B: 8}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Telescoping Series</strong>: Rationalizing fractional terms containing radical differences often reveals a pattern where internal adjacent terms eliminate each other.</li></ul></div>
